Roles are symbolic enum variants, not wire numbers. The wire payload reconstructs precisely these edges. Existing InHeader, InContext, InSpace, AdmissibleRegion, Premise and ConclusionObject roles are reused. Position gaps, duplicate positions, wrong kinds, wrong headers, mismatched parameter scopes, foreign handles and forward references fail before publication. Edges do not imply a theorem merely by existing.

**Definition (Product regions and complex operations)** · `def:nrz:cas-solving-design-regions`

Extend `AmbientDomain` with `Product { coordinates: Handle }`; reuse the existing RegionExpr::GuardTrue, GuardFalse, Union, Meet, Difference, Empty and Ambient constructors. GuardTrue means the predicate is defined and true within its base region. It is a first-class parameter or solution region with the same context/cover machinery, not a sampled grid. Existing interval decision rules reject product ambients unless their schema explicitly supports them. Total zero/nonzero partition applies only to a proved-defined scalar expression; a real three-way sign partition additionally requires realness. Definedness failures create excluded regions with evidence, not an unmentioned third truth value.

Add scalar `Operator::Conjugate`, `RealPart`, `ImagPart` and `RootValue { slot: u32 }`. A dedicated `Draft::RootValue { header: Reference, selection: Reference, slot: u32 }` constructs the scalar Expression with its explicit SelectedBy dependency; the selected handle is not hidden inside an unvalidated operator payload. Add `Operator::ExactCoefficient` and `Draft::ExactCoefficient { header: Reference, value: Reference, map: Reference }` to embed a CoefficientValue into a scalar Expression through an explicit map. This is the graph bridge for Gaussian literals and evaluated tower values. Conjugate preserves the complex scalar sort; RealPart/ImagPart return real scalars and use an explicit real-to-complex embedding when reassembled. Existing `AlgebraicRoot` is a requested presentation and is elaborated into RootRelation/RootSelection before a solve proof. Existing `Set { complete }` remains syntax intent only; new solvers return SolutionSet objects and typed judgments. Complex ordering is invalid as a mathematical request; a real restriction and a separate ordering proof are required. A real request is lifted to a separately constructed complex header through explicit declaration/coefficient maps, then restricted back by solve.real-restrict. The checker transports each used hypothesis through that map and proves or retains its image; equal display names never justify borrowing a theorem from a different context. New complex rules do not weaken the existing scoped-premise checks.

