**Definition (Policy, availability and dominance)** · `def:nrz:cas-solving-design-policy`

The logical v9 weight vector consists of the unchanged 59 logical v8 key/weight pairs plus the 18 rows above, sorted by ASCII key; total 77. logical v9 requires exactly this immutable vector; logical versions below 9 are rejected by the new service. Every weight is positive, and each accepted primitive application pays its weight once per occurrence in the proof DAG accounting contract; sharing never changes the theorem. Certificate/work limits are independent of this preference cost. Schema availability is separate from policy: the foundation may register exact types and weights with a checker returning Unavailable, but no producer transition advertises that schema and replay rejects it until its implementation and tests land. The active-schema bitmap is part of service capability identity, cache keys and acceptance audit; the witness carries only its exact used-rule manifest, and it is never supplied as proof. This allows independently green implementation commits without fake successful checkers or repeated renumbering. Released cubic acceptance enables tags 0 through 14 and tag 16, including the identity family for the zero polynomial; later lanes enable 15 and 17. Lane E adds automatic family production using the already checked tag 16.

A solve search state contains the exact problem, requested goal/form, full context and residual set, coefficient region, tower/component maps, joint selections, proven membership/coverage facts, pending obligations, candidate seed cursor, derivation DAG and usage. Its dominance key includes all of these semantic fields, active-schema bitmap and policy/version; ordering within an unordered fact set is canonical. Only states with identical keys and identical coverage strength compare resource/cost vectors. Compare accumulated positive cost, unresolved obligations, tower product degree, pending component count, maximum expression size and work componentwise; a strictly worse vector can be dropped. Candidates, local families, complete algebraic answers and complete radical answers are distinct strengths, not interchangeable size scores. Different hypotheses, parameter strata, root orientations or denominator obligations never dominate one another merely because their printed expressions coincide.

The deterministic scheduler uses existing cost_quota = 4 and progress_quota = 1. Its progress tuple is number of unclosed domain branches, largest remaining polynomial degree, unresolved selection constraints and pending inverse-transform equations, with structural descriptor tie-breaks; it does not contain a cubic-specific expression pattern. Within a polynomial degree it first attempts degree drop and a general translation eliminating the subleading term, then a supported binomial, then cyclic-character seeds in increasing k and power, then set-partition seeds in lexicographic restricted-growth-string order. An orbit larger than or equal to the original degree is recorded and skipped before expanding its resolvent. Smaller resolvents recurse through the same service; recursion must strictly lower degree. Recovery solves the degree-generic character matrix by linear.equivalent. Pairing constraints are discovered by low-total-degree products of character seeds in increasing exponent-vector order and checked symmetric reductions; they are never supplied as a stored cubic recipe.

For degree n the cyclic seed is Σⱼ ζₙ^(kj)yⱼ for k = 1,…,n − 1. Formal root variables occupy the bound Polynomial frame; they are not extra adjoined computational tower levels. Vieta hypotheses are explicitly scoped and eliminated in the coefficient substitution proof before the radical construction, whose coefficient base remains the original domain. Powers 1 through n/gcd(n,k) are offered in increasing order; the exponent bound follows from the cyclic character order. All S_n orbit generators are adjacent transpositions; closure is checked on the full finite orbit. For n = 3 the two characters, powers 1,2,3 and six permutations are reached by this schedule, and the first lower-degree orbit has size two. The degree-two recursive request uses the same translation and binomial method, yielding completing-the-square as a checked lemma. The product of the two degree-one characters is the first nontrivial cross-character product. Its checked value provides the linked selection; the inverse character matrix supplies the roots. These are consequences of the generic seed order and arithmetic, not production lookup constants.

**Rule (Blind discovery audit)** · `rule:nrz:cas-solving-design-blind`

Pin the source predicate a x³ + b x² + c x + d = 0 with independent coefficient declarations, all coefficient strata and ambient C; use an empty formula/lemma cache. The audit records the exact source revision, schema manifest, active bitmap, logical v9, full limits, seed enumeration, orbit cardinalities, coefficient operation counts, accepted primitive applications and witness digest. The derivation must produce the shift, symmetric reductions, resolvent, linked choices, inverse transform and full product in (`rule:nrz:cas-solving-acceptance`). A test oracle containing a reference formula is loaded only after the search returns; producer modules and fixtures reachable before that point may not contain its constants or identities. No cached quadratic formula is permitted initially; a newly replayed generic degree-two derivation may subsequently be reused with an explicit substitution proof. Rename coefficients and reverse the primitive-root orientation in separate tests; the transported proved set must agree, while byte identity is required only for identical pinned input, capability, policy and limits.

