RealRestriction transport pairs encode the real reference followed by the complex reference. Declaration endpoints are free expressions qualified by their actual header and coordinate product, in coordinate order; display names have no role. The directed map is an independently checked embedding between the exact problem coefficient domains. Each used-condition row encodes its source premise slot, source kind byte (0 Hypothesis, 1 Requirement, 2 Residual), index within that premise's canonical condition collection, context pair, region pair, predicate pair and optional target-context discharge premise slot. Source hypothesis identities and source obligation scopes are reconstructed from that exact earlier premise. The target predicate supplies its checked transported scope; interpretation and definedness requirements are retained. Missing discharge preserves an unresolved source condition as a target guarded residual. Every actually used foreign condition has exactly one row; none is borrowed by display name. Both coefficient covers and every retained or rejected branch retain their exact paired regions. All vectors are bounded before allocation, and all referenced endpoints are ordered graph dependencies. This mandatory subtype-13 payload replaces the incomplete table row in the sole current NRZCAS03 format, agreeing with the cross-header transport requirement of (`def:nrz:cas-solving-design-regions`).

A premise slot is an index into the containing Application's ordered earlier-premise vector; it is not a serialized truth value. All referenced identities must have precisely the endpoints, domains and residual strengths reconstructed by that checker. Mode-specific optional fields use explicit option bytes; missing required fields, extra irrelevant fields, mismatched vector lengths and trailing payload bytes fail. Certificate row order is semantic unless the schema explicitly calls it a set. Splitting-chain, row-operation, generator and reaching-word lengths consume the appropriate new counters as well as payload/work limits. A permutation is a full bijective vector of length n; checking its bijection and each action-table entry is charged.

**Rule (Canonical replay and bounded decoding)** · `rule:nrz:cas-solving-design-replay`

Canonical ordering is the existing structural Kahn order: admit a ready record only after every semantic dependency, compute its full descriptor with already assigned child wire indices, canonicalize its declared sets and choose the lexicographically least ready descriptor. Exact duplicate structural records coalesce. Never use store tokens, insertion serials, hash-table order, object addresses or producer discovery order to break ties. Preserve coordinate, binder, tower, matrix, factor-with-multiplicity, permutation-word and premise order. Canonicalize branch/cover sets together with their aligned certificate rows, so changing a cover order cannot detach its proof. A witness re-encodes byte-for-byte after fresh replay.

Before reserving any vector, the decoder checks remaining bytes, count times minimum element size, the field's limit and global payload/record limits, using checked arithmetic. Before bigint construction it checks component/intermediate byte bounds; before recursive-looking objects it validates bounded acyclic references and uses an explicit work stack. It rejects unknown magic, tags, schemas, fields, modes or rules; unsupported theorem schemas do not become syntax-only proof successes. Validate subtype counts, sort/scope/header/domain compatibility, reduced fractions, monic/reduced tower representations and actual arithmetic budgets. Independently replay each application and compare the complete expected judgment, including original predicate, coordinate order, coefficient field, parameter/solution regions, selections, guards, multiplicity and requested presentation. Publish the imported closure atomically only after canonical re-encoding and expected-claim agreement. A changed selection predicate, component map, coefficient field, omitted branch, false rank, lost denominator or inflated coverage is caught even if the byte stream is otherwise well formed.

## Executable slices and refusal evidence · `sec:nrz:cas-solving-design-tests`

**Definition (Later producer boundaries)** · `def:nrz:cas-solving-design-later-producers`

The rational-polynomial producer clears rational denominators with checked multipliers, removes content, extracts rational roots using the rational-root theorem and exact candidate substitution, and reuses square-free product/gcd evidence. Enumerate divisors under work and integer-size limits; a failed enumeration is Exhausted. A remainder without a factorization certificate is Unclassified, even if no rational root was found. It may be represented by complete algebraic-root relations; it is never labeled irreducible by default. Rational-equation solving retains every denominator reached in the original occurrence walk and proves membership in that strict domain after numerator solving. Real isolation initially admits rational-coefficient univariate square-free support with multiplicity transfer; algebraic-coefficient or general parameter-dependent ordering returns RealIsolationUnavailable unless an existing supplied theorem fits the exact schema.

The exact-linear producer performs bounded fraction-free or unit-checked elimination with a deterministic leftmost pivot order. Every symbolic pivot partitions the current parameter region into zero/nonzero cases unless a premise decides it. It returns ranks of A and [A,b], contradiction rows on inconsistent branches, and one family parameter for each nonpivot unknown on consistent branches. Recovery maps read the free coordinates from an arbitrary solution, proving full family coverage. An inconsistent branch is a complete empty solution set. For an underdetermined system, a particular solution alone is a partial answer. No numerical rank tolerance, singular-value computation or floating matrix certificate enters these judgments; certified numerical execution remains with nrz-matrix.

For local branches, the source neighborhood must contain the base point and be open in the stated finite-dimensional real or complex product. Regularity premises use closed named-predicate contracts `cas.solve.real-c1` and `cas.solve.holomorphic`: arguments are the ordered component expressions of F, and the PredicateFact region fixes the product coordinate order and neighborhood. `cas.solve.open-neighborhood` takes the ordered base-point scalar entries in that same region. A checker accepts these only from explicitly identified context hypotheses, existing sealed theorems with exactly those objects, or the finite polynomial/open-product subcase of solve.local-branch; an arbitrary named predicate with a similar display name is insufficient. Such context assumptions remain used hypotheses of the local conclusion. The polynomial subcase proves regularity by induction over constants, coordinates, addition and multiplication; the ambient whole product is open, and explicitly supplied rational open boxes are checked by exact inequalities. Unsupported neighborhood descriptions remain pending.

The implicit-function theorem concludes existence of smaller neighborhoods; it does not promise that a supplied large neighborhood already has uniqueness throughout. LocalBranch therefore stores existential neighborhood binders unless an additional quantitative/local-cover proof supplies explicit regions. A derivative statement shares precisely that existential scope or the explicitly verified smaller region. For holomorphic F, retain the complex-linear Jacobian structure when invoking the real theorem. The base equation and square unknown Jacobian are checked exactly. Use the existing scalar/Jacobian service where its field is admitted; for complex polynomial components, the local checker independently verifies formal derivatives and holomorphic polynomial regularity under its new schema, without broadening old v1 derivative rules. Then prove Dφ = −(D_y F)⁻¹D_t F; the inverse-function case is the special problem F(x,y) = f(y) − x. Singular base Jacobians, missing C1/holomorphic premises and global claims return the precise unresolved/refusal reason, without a fabricated neighborhood radius.

**Decision (Four existing test layers)** · `dec:nrz:cas-solving-design-test-layout`

Use inline module-private unit tests only for inaccessible local internals, crate tests in `src/tests/`, public contract tests in `tests/conformance/`, and complete client lifecycles in `tests/integration/`. Public directories remain modules of the single `tests/public.rs` target; shared setup lives in `src/testing/` under the existing public/private harness separation. Place each assertion in the outermost layer that can express it. Test inventory labels retain their location-derived areas. Independent expected mathematics uses small exact integer/Fraction-style arithmetic and displayed factor/derivative identities, never the producer or replay checker under test. The following table assigns evidence to each implementation unit; no new test dependency or copied external suite is needed.

