For the blind examples, independently multiply the final factors and differentiate the original polynomial. Bombelli must check (2 + i)³ = 2 + 11i, uv = 5 and roots 4, −2 ± √3. The casus example x³ − 3x + 1 must check the cyclotomic relation t⁶ + t³ + 1 for a ninth root of unity, the three rational sign intervals and irreducibility by the rational-root test in degree three; no floating sample certifies equality or completeness. The repeated example must check (x − 1)²(x + 2), gcd/Bezout and derivative multiplicities. The p = 0 example must check (x + 2)(x² − 2x + 4) = x³ + 8 without dividing by a zero U. The general symbolic test checks the original a,b,c,d polynomial after the affine shift and retains every coefficient stratum. The impossibility of an all-real radical tower for the casus example is mathematical commentary from the concept's cited theorem, not an executable GenericGaloisObstruction result in this release.

Every primitive has one valid supplied-certificate fixture before its producer is enabled, plus a false-premise/certificate mutation and fresh-store replay. Every new cumulative/peak limit has a small exactly measured boundary fixture. Public success tests also inspect the witness's residuals, used hypotheses, solution strength and presentation; checking only a pretty-printed formula is insufficient. Existing scalar, piecewise and distribution tests remain required after the format replacement. The four-layer inventory and gate requirements follow (`dec:nrz:cas-piecewise-design-test-layout`) and (`rule:nrz:required-verification`). No Rust tests or repository gate are claimed to have run in this documentation round.

## Implementation lanes and handoffs · `sec:nrz:cas-solving-design-delivery`

**Procedure (Green commits with disjoint module ownership)** · `alg:nrz:cas-solving-design-lanes`

Each lane below is a two-to-three-hour implementation round with one to three named commits, each independently gated. Times are estimates including targeted tests and one correction pass; an unmet checker or acceptance stays explicit if the estimate is missed. Scratch commits are folded into the listed semantic commits. Every enabled producer is preceded by its replay checker and canonical certificate tests. No unavailable checker returns success. The metering correction titled `perf(cas): meter walks and admissions by the work they do` is a prerequisite supplied by the parallel algebra-removal lane, not work assigned to these lanes.

G owns all common hooks once: it creates the solving facade, closed models, dispatch, empty unavailable leaf modules, all new limit fields and static schema descriptions, and declarations for each lane's test/harness modules. G commit 1 creates all leaf placeholders and test-module declarations, then hands those files to their leaf owners; G commits 2 and 3 edit only common hooks. Later lanes fill their own leaf modules and return their capability availability through the already-declared facade functions. An unavailable leaf has an explicit tested Unavailable result, no transition generator and no accepting theorem stub. This is an implementation staging device; NRZCAS03 decoding still rejects any proof that requires an unavailable checker. The fixed logical v9 manifest describes all 18 schemas, while availability determines which can actually check or execute. Each later lane changes its own availability function only when its supplied-certificate tests pass.

