G's common-hook ownership is `src/lib.rs`, `model.rs`, `syntax.rs`, `task.rs`, `request.rs`, `request_model.rs`, `proof_model.rs`, `proof_view.rs`, `proof_storage.rs`, `scoped_check.rs`, `statement.rs`, `statement_check.rs`, `rule.rs`, `rule_check.rs`, `cost.rs`, `budget.rs`, `limits.rs`, `graph.rs`, `descriptor.rs`, `validation.rs`, `domain_model.rs`, `domain_encoding.rs`, `domain_admission.rs`, the `wire_*.rs` facade files, `encoding.rs`, `search.rs` and `service.rs`, only for the new typed hooks. Include `context_admission.rs` if product-region scope validation requires its hook; this remains G-owned. G replaces the current policy lookup scan with a keyed policy index as part of its cost.rs/State hook, so pinning logical v9 touches only the requested key and performed comparisons. G also declares the solve_* modules once in `src/tests/mod.rs`, `tests/conformance/mod.rs`, `tests/integration/mod.rs`, `src/testing/mod.rs` and `src/testing/public.rs`. Leaf ownership includes its own internal mod.rs files and inline tests. N's backing edit uses existing Budget APIs, so G's new counters do not create a merge conflict in number.rs. The metering lane's graph/traversal changes must land before G starts editing those common hooks.

N commit 1 and G commit 1 can begin concurrently: N edits existing numeric backing/dependencies, while G creates shared representation and dispatch. After G commit 1 hands off the numeric placeholder and test declarations, N commit 2 fills its numeric leaf while G commits 2 and 3 edit only common hooks; no file is edited concurrently by both lanes. After both, T lands. B and O can then run concurrently because their checker/producer/test subdirectories are disjoint. C requires both. D, E and F can run concurrently after C: D owns rational/isolation production, E owns linear-family production and F owns local analysis, all consuming the frozen interfaces and B's common linear/family checkers. No parallel lane edits shared registry, policy, limit, facade or test-declaration files. A necessary interface change returns to the common-hook owner in a serialized correction commit before dependent lanes resume; it is not patched independently in several branches.

The dependency critical path is approximately 15 hours using the longest lane in each parallel wave (G/N, T, B/O, C, D/E/F), plus external gates and correction rounds; the sum of lane estimates is 24.5 hours. These are staffing estimates, not a reason to defer a missing accepted capability or relax a proof check. Each commit's verification includes appropriate tests from its row, all existing affected services and the repository gate of record. Formula generation and mathematical replay are tested independently. Common documentation/census/registry synchronization is coordinated by the owner at release boundaries; no implementation lane edits protected parallel-lane documents without that handoff.

**Rule (Release findings and scope boundaries)** · `rule:nrz:cas-solving-design-findings`

The existing CAS concept/design need narrow follow-up corrections for executable Solve payloads, exact-complex support, num-rational backing, typed solution judgments and sole NRZCAS03; the piecewise design needs the product-region bridge and policy v9 reference. Existing crate README/census and owner/linter inventories need their normal implementation-time updates and correct test labels. The metering correction is an explicit prerequisite and must replace old store-copy/walk accounting in those descriptions. The current cost.rs::pin_policy also scans graph records; G must use the keyed lookup specified above so an unrelated stored object does not trigger a full-store policy scan. These are owner-coordinated follow-ups, not edits made by this design round. No architecture, units, problem-data-schema, plan, root README, docgen README or linter registry change is authorized here.

The delivered scope includes the new design template/render and document-manifest row only, atop the concept's supplied mechanical corrections. It does not add code, dependencies, parser syntax, a legacy decoder, general factorization, specialized Galois algorithms, broad nonlinear solving, numerical matrix execution or a global inverse theorem. The generic quintic impossibility checker remains explicitly deferred; all other advertised executable slices have concrete checker, wire, limit and acceptance assignments above. The mathematical references retain the verification caveats recorded in the concept; no new external implementation, bibliography lookup or test suite was consulted.
